m68k: take a pointer return value from the register the compiler uses.

libffi's m68k ffi_call reads a pointer return from %a0. That is the
m68k SVR4 convention, where a function returning a pointer leaves it in
%a0 and the epilogue copies it to %d0 as well, so that callers which
never saw a declaration still find it.

This toolchain does not do that. m68k-elf gcc 15.2.0 compiles

    const char *picker(const char *a, const char *b, int w)
    { return w ? b : a; }

to

    tst.l 12(%sp) / jeq .L2 / move.l 8(%sp),4(%sp)
    .L2: move.l 4(%sp),%d0 / rts

-- %d0 only, and %a0 is left holding whatever it held. So ffi_call
returned a plausible-looking address that was not the function's:
0x100004f0, somewhere in the program's own text, instead of the string
it had asked for. Nothing failed; the wrong pointer simply came back.

Reading %d0 is correct for both conventions, because SVR4 puts the
value in %d0 as well. That is why this patch does not need to ask
which one is in force, and why it is safe on a target that does use
%a0. __MINT__ already took this path; the #ifdef is now redundant and
is removed rather than extended.

The CLOSURE side needs nothing: it already writes the pointer to %a0
and %d0 both.

--- a/src/m68k/sysv.S
+++ b/src/m68k/sysv.S
@@ -163,11 +163,13 @@
 retpointer:
 	btst	#5,%d2
 	jbeq	retstruct1
-#ifdef __MINT__
+	| %d0, not %a0. The SVR4 convention returns a pointer in %a0 and
+	| copies it to %d0; m68k-elf gcc uses %d0 alone and leaves %a0
+	| holding something else entirely. %d0 holds the value under
+	| both, so this needs no test for which is in force. Reading
+	| %a0 here did not fail -- it returned an address from the
+	| program's own text as though it were the result.
 	move.l	%d0,(%a1)
-#else
-	move.l	%a0,(%a1)
-#endif
 	jbra	epilogue
 
 retstruct1:
